                                                                                <% const faqData=[ {
                                                                                    title: "What is asset tokenization?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "Asset tokenization is the process of converting the ownership rights of a real-world or digital asset into tokens on a blockchain. Each token represents a defined share of the asset, its value, income rights, and ownership record stored transparently on-chain and transferable without intermediaries."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "What is the difference between asset tokenization and an NFT?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "An NFT represents a unique, one-of-a-kind item with no divisibility. Digital asset tokenization typically involves fungible tokens where each token is identical and interchangeable, representing fractional ownership of an underlying asset like a property or fund."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "What is RWA tokenization?" ,
                                                                                    content: "RWA stands for Real World Asset. RWA tokenization platform development refers specifically to converting ownership rights of physical or traditional financial assets, such as real estate, commodities, bonds, and private equity, into blockchain-based digital tokens."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "Which assets can be tokenized?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "Virtually any asset with defined ownership rights and economic value can be tokenized, including real estate, private equity, bonds, commodities, art, intellectual property, carbon credits, infrastructure assets, and more."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "What blockchain is best for asset tokenization?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "The right blockchain depends on your asset class, regulatory environment, and investor base. Ethereum and Polygon are the most common for regulated security tokens. BNB Chain suits lower-cost, high-volume platforms. Avalanche and Solana suit platforms needing faster settlement. We build multi-chain platforms so you are not locked to one network."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "Is digital asset tokenization legal?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "Tokenized assets that represent ownership or economic rights are typically classified as securities in most jurisdictions and must comply with local securities laws. The platform must include KYC/AML investor onboarding, transfer restrictions, and jurisdiction-specific compliance controls. We build all of this into every platform we develop."
                                                                                    }, {
                                                                                    title: "How is ownership of a tokenized asset recorded?"
                                                                                    ,
                                                                                    content: "Ownership is recorded on the blockchain in real time. Every token transfer, purchase, and distribution is written permanently to the chain, creating a transparent, tamper-proof ownership record that updates automatically with every transaction."
                                                                                    }, {
